Most teams know the CI loop: push a change, wait for a cloud runner to install
the project, run the checks, and report a status on the pull request. The cloud
runner gives everyone a shared answer. It is slower and more expensive than a
warm checkout on a developer's machine, but it gives the team a place to trust.

Agents make the cost of that loop easier to see. An agent may run the same
checks many times while it is working. It already has the source, dependencies,
and tools on disk, so it can get useful feedback locally in seconds. Sending
every attempt back to a fresh cloud runner adds setup time and another CI bill.

That does not mean a local green terminal should replace CI. The local machine
may have run the wrong command, reported only the checks that passed, or tested
a checkout that is no longer the code that will merge. The question is whether
the work already done locally can help with the merge decision without asking
GitHub to trust an unexplained report.

{% mermaid label="The current feedback loop and the gap a proof needs to close" %}
```mermaid
flowchart LR
  A["Agent edits a pull request"] --> B["Warm local checkout<br/>fast feedback"]
  A --> C["Cloud CI rebuilds<br/>and checks"]
  C --> D["Shared merge signal"]
  B -. "useful work, but<br/>not yet verifiable" .-> D
```
{% /mermaid %}

There is a second problem even when the local run was completely honest. CI on
a pull request usually checks the pull request's head. The code that lands may
also include newer changes from `main`. If those two snapshots differ, a green
result for the first one does not say anything certain about the second. [The
merge-queue post](/blog/merge-queue/) explains how thwip builds and pins that
integrated candidate.

So thwip asks a practical question: what if the local work could produce a
clear, signed claim about exactly what it checked? GitHub could then compare the
claim with the code under review. If the team wants a second execution, GitHub
could still run the checks itself. The proof would not replace trust; it would
make the remaining trust visible.

## What the proof says

A proof is a signed statement about a concrete result. In plain language, it
says something like:

> These required checks passed on this exact snapshot of the repository, under
> this project's rules, using this check recipe, and this device key signed the
> statement.

The exact snapshot matters. Git calls its snapshot of file names, file contents,
and file permissions a [tree](https://git-scm.com/book/en/v2/Git-Internals-Git-Objects).
Git gives that tree a digest—a compact fingerprint of its contents. Change a
file, add a file, or change its permissions and the digest changes. A proof
that names the digest is therefore tied to the files that were checked, not
just to a branch name or a commit message.

thwip carries the proof on the commit in a `thwip-proof: v1...` Git trailer. The
trailer contains a signed statement, not a human-readable log. It uses two
standard building blocks:

- [in-toto](https://in-toto.io/) is a format for describing what happened to a
  software artifact as it moved through a process. thwip uses the Git tree as
  the artifact and records the checks that were claimed for it.
- [DSSE](https://github.com/secure-systems-lab/dsse) is a standard signed
  envelope. It keeps the statement's exact bytes together with the signature
  and the identifier of the key that signed them. If somebody edits the claim,
  signature verification fails.

The statement also gives the claim enough context to check. It names the tree
and the base it was built from, so a verifier can tell which code and which
version of `main` were involved. It names the policy, which says what checks
were required. It names the pipeline, a fingerprint of the check recipe, so a
change to how the checks run is visible. It records the runner and timing, the
individual suite results, and the signing key's identity.

Those details do not make a local machine trustworthy by themselves. They make
the claim specific enough for another system to compare it with the change,
the active policy, and the required checks.

{% mermaid label="How local work becomes a GitHub merge check" %}
```mermaid
flowchart TD
  A["Run required checks on<br/>one exact Git tree"] --> B["Write a claim about<br/>tree, results, and rules"]
  B --> C["Sign the claim with<br/>an enrolled device key"]
  C --> D["GitHub App reads the<br/>claim and the Git tree"]
  D --> E{"Do they match?"}
  E -- "no" --> F["Required check fails"]
  E -- "yes" --> G["Required check passes"]
  G --> H["Queue or GitHub may merge"]
```
{% /mermaid %}

## How GitHub verifies it

The thwip GitHub App does not run repository code to make this decision. It
reads the commit and GitHub's view of the pull request, verifies the DSSE
signature using the enrolled signing key, and checks that the key belongs to a
GitHub user with enough repository permission.

It then compares the claim with the change under review: the tree digest, the
base, the active policy, the check recipe, and the required suite results. A
proof for one tree cannot satisfy a check for another. If the pull request head
has changed since the proof was made, the mismatch is visible and the required
`thwip` check fails. When a queue asks a device to check an integrated
candidate, the same tree comparison happens between the queue's assignment and
the device's receipt before the merge.

This is the useful boundary. The App can verify who signed the bytes and what
those bytes claim. It can verify that the claim names the code and checks the
policy expects. It does not pretend that it watched every command on the local
machine.

## What the signature does—and does not—establish

The signature establishes that a particular enrolled key signed an unchanged
statement. The statement says that named checks passed on a named Git tree
under a named policy and pipeline. GitHub can verify those relationships before
it allows the required check to pass.

The signature does not establish that the checks really ran. It does not prove
that the machine was free of malware, that its network or cache behaved as
claimed, or that the person holding the key did not sign a false result. A
compromised device can produce a perfectly valid signature over a bad claim.

That is why thwip treats a proof as a precise claim, not as magic. The claim is
stronger than terminal output because it is bound to an exact tree and checked
against GitHub's records. It is weaker than a second execution that GitHub
performs itself.

## Four ways to pay for confidence

Different projects will make that trade differently. thwip puts the choices in
four profiles:

| Profile | What supplies the evidence | Trade-off |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `fast` | Reuse a signed proof on the pull request head. | Lowest cost and latency; the integrated candidate is not run again. |
| `fresh` | A device runs the queue's exact integrated candidate and signs a receipt. | No cloud rerun; the project trusts and needs that runner. |
| `remote` | A pinned GitHub Actions verifier runs the candidate. | GitHub supplies the second party; each candidate costs CI time. |
| `audited` | Run on a device, then rerun the required checks in GitHub Actions. | Strongest evidence, with both execution costs. |

`fast` is honest about its limit: it proves that the head proof is valid, not
that the post-merge candidate was executed. `fresh` goes further. The queue
gives a device a signed assignment naming the exact candidate tree and required
checks. The device verifies that assignment, runs the checks, and signs a
receipt naming the same tree. The control plane accepts the receipt only when
it answers that assignment. `remote` moves the execution to GitHub. `audited`
asks for both kinds of evidence.

The cost difference is practical. In 18 successful candidate verifications in
this repository, GitHub Actions spent a median 36 seconds setting up the
runner, checking out the code, installing dependencies, and building workspace
packages before a suite started. The already-warm local suite results had a
median of 4.5 seconds. Those are measurements from this repository, not a
promise about every project, but they show why a team may want local work to
count.

GitHub also bills runner time by runner class and rounds jobs up to whole
minutes. [Its published rates](https://docs.github.com/en/billing/reference/actions-minute-multipliers)
show the cloud cost. `fresh` avoids that rerun but asks the project to trust a
device. `remote` pays for a shared execution environment. `audited` pays for
both. thwip makes that choice explicit instead of hiding it inside a green
check.

## What this does not solve

Proofs do not replace code review. A passing suite says that named checks passed
on a named tree. It says nothing about whether the product is secure or whether
the tests cover the important behavior.

Proofs also do not remove environment differences. If a suite needs a cloud
service, a platform-specific runner, or a secret that cannot exist on a local
machine, keep that suite remote. A project can combine remote suites with a
local profile, and `audited` can add a full GitHub rerun when the stronger
reference is worth its cost.

The hardest remaining case is a compromised signing device under `fast`. With
no second execution, there is no mechanism that can detect the first false
proof. `fresh` narrows the claim by requiring a receipt for the integrated
candidate; `remote` and `audited` add GitHub's independent execution. None of
them makes a stolen private key harmless.

That boundary is the point of the model. A proof tells GitHub exactly what was
claimed, which code it covered, who signed it, and what the policy required. It
does not ask GitHub to believe a sentence merely because a terminal printed it.

